  "response": "I've created `/app/filter.py`. Run it as `python3 /app/filter.py file.html` and it cleans the file in place. It cuts out only the dangerous pieces of text; everything else stays byte-for-byte the same. A file with nothing to remove isn't rewritten at all. I tested it on one sample page in the container, built from common attack tricks plus normal markup. Every attack in it was removed, and the doctype, title, `<style>`, comments, table, header, safe links, inline styles and accented text came through unchanged. Nothing beyond that one file was tested.\n\n**What gets removed:**\n- **Script elements:** `<script>` blocks, including everything inside them, in any letter case. A `<script>` with no closing tag is removed through to the end of the file.\n- **Event handlers:** every attribute starting with `on`, such as `onload`, `onerror` or `onclick`, including in run-together forms like `<img/src=x/onerror=...>`.\n- **Dangerous links:** attribute values starting with `javascript:` or `vbscript:`, even when disguised with HTML entities or whitespace. It also removes `data:` links except for ordinary image types like PNG or JPEG. SVG animation values and `<meta>` refresh targets that point to `javascript:` are removed too.\n- **Embedded pages:** an iframe `srcdoc` that contains script.\n- **Dangerous CSS:** `style` attributes or `<style>` blocks using `expression(`, `behavior:`, `-moz-binding` or `javascript:`.\n- **Plugin tags:** the `<object>`, `<embed>` and `<applet>` tags themselves are removed, but the fallback content inside them is kept.\n\n**Edge cases it handles:**\n- It reads the page the way a browser does, so text inside comments isn't treated as code.\n- Content inside `<style>`, `<title>`, `<noscript>`, `<textarea>`, `<iframe>` and similar is also checked as if it were HTML. This catches tricks like `<noscript><p title=\"</noscript><img onerror=...>\">` and HTML hidden inside an SVG `<style>`.\n- It runs repeatedly until nothing changes, so a removal can't join two leftover pieces into a new attack (e.g. `<<script></script>img onerror=...>`).\n\n**Where the output differs from the input:**\n- When an attribute is removed, the space before it stays, so you get `<a >` rather than `<a>`.\n- It errs on the side of removing too much. Any attribute named `on…` is removed, and so is a tag left unclosed at the end of the file if it looks suspicious (browsers ignore such tags anyway).\n- If a `<title>` or `<textarea>` contains literal markup like `<script>` or `onclick=` as text, that text is stripped too.",
